<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="sec-6-3-piecewise-linear" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title component="not-workbook">Piecewise-linear functions</title>
	<title component="workbook">Piecewise-linear functions - Practice exercises</title>

	<subsection component="not-workbook">
    <title>Late parking ticket</title>
        <p>
          Mallory is having a miserable time. A few weeks back she forgot to pay
          the parking meter and the city issued a parking ticket. The parking
          ticket was for $45 and was due within 30 days. After that, the city
          imposes a late fee of $10. Bummer.
        </p>

        <p>
          But Mallory is curious <ndash/> how can she write an equation to
          describe the cost of her parking ticket when it changes like that? She
          knows variables are
          <md>
            <mrow>D \amp= \text{ time since she got the ticket (days) }\sim \text{ indep}</mrow>
            <mrow>C \amp= \text{ cost of parking ticket (\$) }\sim \text{ dep}</mrow>
          </md>
          The starting amount (intercept) is $45. What's the rate of change
          (slope)? During the first 30 days, the price does not change. Since
          that rate of change is constant, we have a linear equation and the
          slope is $0/day. The equation for the first 30 days is
          <md>
            C = 45 + 0D
          </md>
          But <m>0D = 0</m>, so really
          <md>
            C = 45
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          By the way, there is a name for this type of function. It's called a
          <alert>constant function</alert>. Meaning the value of the ticket is
          constant, at least during those first 30 days.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now, after 30 days, the price jumps to <m>45+10 = \$55</m>. The
          equation for the price then is
          <md>
            P = 55
          </md>
          another constant function.
        </p>

        <p>
          There's a notation we can use to describe <m>C</m> as a function of
          <m>D</m> that includes both values. We write
          <md>
            C = \left\{ \begin{array}{cl}45 \amp \text{if } 0 \le D \le 30 \\ 55 \amp \text{if } D \gt 30\end{array} \right.
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          The way we read this notation is: if the number of days <m>D</m> is
          between 0 and 30, then the ticket costs <m>P=</m> $45 but if the
          number of days is over 30, then the ticket costs <m>C=</m> $55.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection component="not-workbook">
        <title>Piecewise-linear functions and their graphs</title>

        <p>
          When our equation has two (or more) pieces, it's called a
          <alert>piecewise-defined function</alert>. In this type of situation
          where both pieces are linear, it's called a
          <alert>piecewise-linear function</alert>.
        </p>

        <p>
          What does the graph of a piecewise-linear function look like? Let's
          make a table of values. Notice any value of <m>D</m> over 30 days is
          gonna cost Mallory $55. Even if she's 2 minutes late, it's gonna cost
          $55. Note that 2 minutes late is 30.0014 days since
          <md>
            2 \text{ min}= \frac{2 \text{ min}}{1}\times \frac{1 \text{ hour}}{60 \text{ min}}\times \frac{1 \text{ day}}{24 \text{ hours}}= 2 \div 60 \div 24 = .001388\ldots \approx .0014 \text{ days}
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          Here are the values we know.
        </p>

        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>D</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>20</cell>
            <cell>30</cell>
            <cell>30.0014</cell>
            <cell>40</cell>
            <cell>50</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>C</m></cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>55</cell>
            <cell>55</cell>
            <cell>55</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          When we draw the graph and connect the dots into lines, we notice two
          things. First, the lines are horizontal. That always happens when the
          function is constant and the slope is 0. Second, there's a jump at 30
          days where the graph isn't connected, which makes sense because the
          cost of the ticket jumps up after 30 days.
        </p>
        <image xml:id="tikz-6-3-graph-1">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=0.2, yscale=0.1]
                    \foreach \y in {10,20,...,60} {
                        \draw[gray!30, very thin] (0,\y) -- (60,\y);
                    }
                    \foreach \x in {5,10,...,60} {
                        \draw[gray!30, very thin] (\x,0) -- (\x,60);
                    }
                    \draw[thick, -\gt ] (0,0) -- (63,0) node[right] {$D$};
                    \draw[thick, -\gt ] (0,0) -- (0,63) node[above] {$C$};
                    \foreach \x in {5,10,...,60} {
                        \draw (\x, 1) -- (\x, -1) node[below] { \x};
                    }
                    \foreach \y in {10,20,...,60} {
                        \draw (0.2, \y) -- (-0.2, \y) node[left] { \y};
                    }
                    \draw[very thick, blue] plot[domain=0:30] (\x, {45});
                    \draw[very thick, blue] plot[domain=30.2:60] (\x, {55});
                    \node[circle, fill=blue, inner sep=1.5pt] at (30, 45) {};
                    \node[circle, draw=blue, fill=white, thick, inner sep=1.5pt] at (30, 55) {};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          Notice how we indicate the values corresponding to <m>D=30</m>. We use
          a solid dot at <m>(30,45)</m> because that's a point on the graph: at
          30 days the parking ticket costs $45. We use an open dot at
          <m>(30,55)</m> because that's not a point on the graph: at 30 days the
          parking ticket is not yet $55.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection component="not-workbook">
        <title>Late rent</title>

        <p>
          Mallory's troubles aren't over. She's also behind on the rent. She
          normally owes <m>900</m> and there's a one week grace period, meaning
          she can pay up to one week late without any late fees. For the next
          week, the late fee is $10/day. After that, the late fee jumps to
          $25/day.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's measure time in days. We have
          <md>
            <mrow>L \amp= \text{ how late the rent is (days)}\sim \text{ indep}</mrow>
            <mrow>R \amp= \text{ rent plus any late fees (\$)}\sim \text{ dep}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          What happens during the first week? For example, when <m>L=0</m> days,
          the rent is $900. When <m>L=4</m> days, we're within the grace period
          ( <m>0 \le L \le 7</m> ) so the rent is still $900. The equation
          during this week is <m>R = 900</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
        What happens during the second week? For example, when <m>L = 10</m>
        days, she's <m>10-7=3</m> days late and we're during the week where it
        costs an extra $10/day ( <m>7\lt L\le14</m> ), so the rent is now
        <md>
            <mrow>R \amp = 900 + (10-7) \times 10 </mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 900 + 3 \times 10 </mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 900 + 30</mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = \$930</mrow>
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
        The number of days late after the 7 day grace period is <m>L-7</m>.
        The equation is
        <md>
        R = 900 + (L-7)10
        </md>
        We can use the distributive property to multiply out to get
        <md>
            <mrow>R \amp = 900 + (L-7) \times 10 </mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 900 + 10L - 70 </mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 830 + 10L</mrow>
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
        What happens during the third week? For example, when <m>L=20</m>
        days, she pays $900 rent plus $10/day for the first 7 days late plus
        $25/day for the next <m>20-14=6</m> days late, so that's a total of
        <md>
            <mrow>R \amp = 900 + 7 \times 10 + (20-14) \times 25</mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 900 + 70 + 6 \times 25</mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 970 + 150</mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = \$1120</mrow>
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
        Ouch! The number of days where she's gonna pay an extra $25/day is
        <m>L -14</m>. The equation is
        <md>
            R = 900 + 10\times7 + 25(L-14)
        </md>
        Again, we can use the distributive property to multiply out to get
        <md>
            <mrow>R \amp = 900 + 10 \times 7 + 25(L - 14)</mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 900 + 70 + 25L -25\times 14 </mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 970 + 25L - 350</mrow>
            <mrow>  \amp = 620 + 25L</mrow>
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          When we put everything together we get
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            R = \left\{ \begin{array}{cl}900 \amp \text{if } 0 \le L \le 7\\ 830 + 10L \amp \text{if } 7 \lt L \le 14\\ 620+25L\amp \text{if } L \gt 14\end{array} \right.
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
        In case you were wondering about part days, Mallory's apartment
        complex prorates the late fees are prorated by the hour (or even by
        the minute). So, for example, if Mallory pays her rent 9 days and 6
        hours late, we would pay $10/day for those 2 days and 6 hours after
        the grace period which is
        <md>
            <mrow>2 \text{ days} + 6 \text{ hours} \amp = 2 \text{ days} + \dfrac{6 \text{ hours}}{1} \times \frac{1 \text{ day}}{24 \text{ hours}}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp 2+6 \div 24</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp 2 + 0.25 = 2.25 \text{ days}</mrow>
        </md>
        and so the late fee would be prorated at
        <m>2.25 \times 10 = \$22.50</m> for a total of
        <m>900 + 22.50 = \$922.50</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's summarize our examples and a few more values in a table.
        </p>

        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>L</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>14</cell>
            <cell>15</cell>
            <cell>20</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>R</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>900</cell>
            <cell>900</cell>
            <cell>930</cell>
            <cell>970</cell>
            <cell>995</cell>
            <cell>1120</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          Now we can draw the graph.
        </p>
        <image xml:id="tikz-6-3-graph-2">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=0.4, yscale=0.6]
                    \foreach \y in {1,2,...,14} {
                        \draw[gray!30, very thin] (0,\y) -- (28,\y);
                    }
                    \foreach \x in {2,4,...,28} {
                        \draw[gray!30, very thin] (\x,0) -- (\x,14);
                    }
                    \draw[thick, -\gt ] (0,0) -- (30,0) node[right] {$L$};
                    \draw[thick, -\gt ] (0,0) -- (0,14.5) node[above] {$R$};
                    \foreach \x in {2,4,...,28} {
                        \draw (\x, 0.2) -- (\x, -0.2) node[below] { \x};
                    }
                    \foreach \y/\lbl in {2/200, 4/400, 6/600, 8/800, 10/1000, 12/1200,14/1400} {
                        \draw (0.2, \y) -- (-0.2, \y) node[left] { \lbl};
                    }
                    \draw[very thick, blue] plot[domain=0:7] (\x, {900/100});
                    \draw[very thick, blue] plot[domain=7:14] (\x, {(830+10*\x)/100});
                    \draw[very thick, blue] plot[domain=14:28] (\x, {(620+25*\x)/100});
                    \node[circle, fill=blue, inner sep=1.5pt] at (7, 9) {};
                    \node[circle, fill=blue, inner sep=1.5pt] at (14, 9.7) {};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
          Notice that there are no jumps in the graph this time. That's because
          when <m>L=7</m> we know <m>R=900</m> but if we were to use the
          equation <m>R=830+10L</m> when <m>L=7</m> instead, we would get
          <m>R = 830+10 \times 7 = 900</m> again. That tells us that the second
          line starts where the first horizontal line ends. Similarly, when
          <m>L=14</m> we use the equation <m>R = 830+10L</m> to get
          <m>R = 830+10*14=970</m> but if we were to use the equation
          <m>R=620+25L</m> when <m>L = 14</m> instead, then we would get
          <m>R=620+25 \times 14 = 970</m> again. This tells us that the third
          line starts where the second line ends. Sometimes we put closed dots
          to emphasize where the formula changes.
        </p>
      </subsection>


